/*
RMSTEST_20 - clumping_factor regimes for TERRAINS
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 3, Sec.6.4's table

QUESTION
Sec.6.4 models guide:1648's four terrain regimes as frontier weight buckets.
Negative is snakey, 0 to 5 irregular, 5 to 25 rounder, above 25 clamped to the
rounder behaviour with no directional streaking. Every one of those boundaries
came from reading prose. None has been measured, and the table is deliberately
non-monotonic, which is a strong claim to make on no evidence.

Note this is the terrain table, NOT the land table. Rev 4 corrected the land
table and left its terrain twin pointing at it, which was the same error one
level down. RMSTEST_21 covers lands separately for that reason.

METHOD
Nine create_terrain commands, one clump each, identical 400 tile budgets,
distinct terrain constants, and clumping_factor stepped across every regime
boundary the table claims. Same map, same run, so seed and map state are shared
and the only difference between the nine patches is the factor.

Read circularity, which is 4*pi*area/perimeter^2, normalised so a disc is 1.0
and a one-tile-wide snake tends toward 0. A solid square reads 0.785. It is
scale free, so the nine are comparable directly.

spacing_to_other_terrain_types 0 everywhere, so the nine do not push each other
around and each one grows into whatever GRASS is left. FOREST and LEAVES are
excluded because forest terrain auto-places a tree object per tile, and BEACH
and the water terrains are excluded because DE paints beach automatically at a
land and water boundary, which is the trap that made the first attempt at item
7c unreadable.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200). Nine clumps of 400 is 3600 tiles of 40000, or 9
percent, so the later commands still have plenty of eligible GRASS.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. Circularity rises with clumping_factor across -5, 0, 3, 5, 8, 15, 20, 25.
2. DIRT at -5 is the lowest of the nine and clearly separated, not merely last.
3. GRASS3 at 40 reads the same as GRASS2 at 25 within run to run noise. That
   is the clamp claim, and it is the one most likely to be wrong, because it
   was reasoned from the absence of a guide sentence rather than from one.
4. The 0 to 5 and 5 to 25 boundaries produce a visible step rather than a
   smooth ramp. If circularity ramps smoothly instead, the bucket model is the
   wrong shape and a continuous weight is closer to the engine.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DIRT
  and once per terrain, or read the terrain histogram first and pick.

WATCH FOR
Patch count per terrain should be 1. Two patches means the clump was split by
ineligible tiles and its circularity is meaningless. Also check each patch is
near 400 tiles. A short patch ran out of eligible GRASS and its shape is a
record of what was left over, not of its clumping factor.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

  /* negative, "extremely snakey" */
  create_terrain DIRT
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 1
    number_of_tiles 400
    clumping_factor -5
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 0
  }

  /* 0 to 5, "more irregular terrain patches" */
  create_terrain DIRT2
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 1
    number_of_tiles 400
    clumping_factor 0
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 0
  }

  create_terrain DIRT3
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 1
    number_of_tiles 400
    clumping_factor 3
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 0
  }

  /* the 5 boundary itself, which the table straddles */
  create_terrain DESERT
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 1
    number_of_tiles 400
    clumping_factor 5
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 0
  }

  /* 5 to 25, "rounder terrain patches". 8 is the LAND default, irregular for
     lands and rounder for terrains, which is exactly the value that a shared
     bucket function would mis-classify. */
  create_terrain PALM_DESERT
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 1
    number_of_tiles 400
    clumping_factor 8
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 0
  }

  create_terrain SNOW
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 1
    number_of_tiles 400
    clumping_factor 15
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 0
  }

  /* 20 is the terrain default (guide:1646, "different than for lands") */
  create_terrain ICE
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 1
    number_of_tiles 400
    clumping_factor 20
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 0
  }

  create_terrain GRASS2
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 1
    number_of_tiles 400
    clumping_factor 25
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 0
  }

  /* above the guide's stated useful range. Sec.6.4 clamps to the 25 behaviour
     and invents no directional regime. This pair is the clamp test. */
  create_terrain GRASS3
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 1
    number_of_tiles 400
    clumping_factor 40
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 0
  }

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
